A||N||3||Will||2.8/3.1/3.4 internal dimensions||||1||
Z||000000||Will||06-13-2001||12:58 PM||||I just read in this thread: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008753.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008753.html</A> <BR>That the 2.8 and 3.1 use the same con rods but the 3.1 has a lower crown height on the pistons. Is that the case?<BR>Dimensionally a 3.4 is a bored out 3.1, right?<P>What about using a 2.8 crank with low crown pistons and custom rods in a 3.4 block? That should net about 3.1 litres, but that engine would have a more favorable rod/stroke ratio than a regular 3.1, allowing higher compression or more boost without increasing the fuel's octane.<P>Any thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||06-13-2001||02:59 PM||||2.8 = 2.8 crank, x rods and x sized pistons<BR>3.1 = 3.1 crank, x rods and x sized pistons (except they have a diff wrist pin placement)<BR>3.4 = 3.1 crank, x rods and larger then x pistons (w/ the same wrist pin placement as 3.1)<P>the 3.4 is a slightly different block, the side walls are thicker to allow for the larger bore.<P>as for a 2.8 crank in a 3.4 block... awesome idea! it'd be expencive tho, and you'd have to theory out some more of the intake. Custom rods and pistons, possible custom heads (to run the compression up nice and high and get some extra flowage) there was a link posted in the "opening a bag of worms(which v8)" thread that had a complete write up one building a 350 out of a 307/327 crank and a 400 block. If I remember right, they were running 11:1 compression at over 300hp on 89 octane gas, but they had aluminum heads. I'd love to try the same kinda build w/ a 3.4 but I don't have that kind of money.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shaun41178||06-13-2001||03:56 PM||||you wouldn't need custom rods if you used a 2.8 crank in a 3.4 block.  You would just need to have custom pistons made to keep the compression ratio up around 9:1  It would be like putting a 289 crank in a 350 block to get a 302.  That used the stock 289 rods but used different pistons for the compression.  First put in the Z28's that thing would rev to 7k all day<P>I have thought of the idea of a 2.8 crank and custom pistons in a original 3.4 DOHC block and DOHC heads.  Talk about rev happy.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||06-13-2001||09:05 PM||||I posted that article about the 400 block with 327 crank. It got 430 HP on 87 octane with 11:1 compression. 400 ftlbs from 2000 rpm<BR>The rod/stroke ratio on that engine was around 2.0:1.<P>What is the rod length for 2.8/3.1/3.4?<BR>What are the strokes for 2.8, 3.1/3.4?<P>The whole point of such a build would be to use longer rods with stock or stock replacement pistons/crank; so, yes, custom rods would be needed. Once rod/stroke ratio gets up over 2.0:1, 9:1 compression on pump gas is child's play, and much less than what could be run.<BR>     One could use F-body 3.4 pistons with GEN III aluminum heads for high compression and much better flow than iron heads. What CR does that yield? <BR>||||reg||1
